# Angelo CLI Operations & Troubleshooting

This rule keeps the agent from improvising on angelo's own setup commands. When
the user asks about installing, updating, or fixing angelo, follow this — don't
hand-edit `mcp.json`, reinstall blindly, or guess at commands.

## Update decision tree — read this first

When the user says "update angelo", **classify the install before taking any
package action**:

1. Run `angelo update --plan`. It is read-only and prints the install mode,
   intended actions, and exact next commands.
2. If it reports a **normal / copied install**, run `angelo update`, then restart
   the editor and MCP servers.
3. If it reports an **editable development checkout**, update in this order:
   `git pull --ff-only` → `angelo update` → restart the editor and MCP servers.
   `angelo update` reconciles managed configuration but intentionally does not
   fetch source code or reinstall the package.
4. Re-run `pip install -e ".[zettelkasten,dashboard]"` only when the pull changed
   dependencies or console-script entry points. Run `angelo kill` first.

**Hard stops for editable installs:**

- Never run `pip install --upgrade angelo` or replace the editable install with a
  copied package.
- Never treat "managed configuration is up to date" as proof that the source
  checkout is current; they are separate states.
- `--force-upgrade` is refused for editable installs. Do not work around that
  protection.

## The commands (what each one is for)

| Command | What it does | When |
|---|---|---|
| `angelo init [--cursor\|--claude] [--with-zettelkasten] [--local-paths]` | Scaffolds editor config (`mcp.json`, the user-global Cursor plugin at `~/.cursor/plugins/local/angelo/`, rules, hooks, skills). | First-time setup in a project. |
| `angelo update [--plan]` | Reconciles the managed rules/hooks/skills and `mcp.json` to match the **installed** package version. On a **normal (copied) install** it also self-upgrades the package first (`pip install --upgrade` from git when newer); on an **editable install** it never runs pip and only reconciles config. `--plan` reports what would happen without changing anything; `--no-upgrade` skips the package step. | To pick up new managed files, or to upgrade + reconcile in one step. |
| `angelo doctor` | Verifies the install: PATH commands, dependencies, config files, onboarding state. Read-only. | Anytime something seems off. |
| `angelo doctor --fix` | Repairs editor launch paths — rewrites `mcp.json` commands to absolute console-script paths and reinstalls/repairs the user-global Cursor plugin. | When MCP servers won't start or do not appear in Customize (see below). |
| `angelo memory http start\|status\|restart\|stop` | Opts Cursor's memory MCP into a managed loopback URL, inspects/restarts that daemon, or restores the exact prior entry. | When memory must restart independently of Cursor; `stop` is rollback. |
| `angelo kill [--dry-run]` | Kills every running angelo process (servers + dashboard/runner children). | Before an editable reinstall (the running servers lock the scripts). |

## "MCP servers won't start" (the most common problem)

The editor launches MCP servers with **its own PATH**, which usually does *not*
include the venv/`Scripts`/`bin` directory where the `angelo-*` commands live.
So a bare command like `angelo-memory` that runs fine in your shell can still
fail to start inside Cursor/Claude Code — especially when angelo is installed in
a virtualenv. Fix it deterministically:

1. Run `angelo doctor` — it reports whether each command resolves and prints the
   absolute paths.
2. Run **`angelo doctor --fix`** — it rewrites the `command` fields in `mcp.json`
   to those absolute paths (env blocks and non-angelo servers are left intact) and
   reinstalls/repairs the user-global Cursor plugin so it points at this
   environment.
3. Restart the editor. On Cursor 3.9+, verify the servers under
   **Customize → MCPs** (populated by the user-global plugin at
   `~/.cursor/plugins/local/angelo/`); older Cursor releases continue loading
   `.cursor/mcp.json` directly.

Do **not** hand-edit `mcp.json` or reinstall the package for this — `--fix` is
the intended repair. Note the rewritten paths are machine-specific, so for a
shared repo each teammate runs `--fix` (or `angelo init --local-paths`) in their
own environment rather than committing the absolute paths.

## "Servers don't appear in Customize → MCPs"

On Cursor 3.9+, the angelo servers show up in **Customize → MCPs** because
`angelo init` installs a **user-global** Cursor plugin at
`~/.cursor/plugins/local/angelo/` — that plugin, not `.cursor/mcp.json`, is what
Cursor's plugin loader enumerates. If the servers are missing from Customize:

**A loaded plugin is not a registered one.** Cursor logs
`loadUserLocalPlugin angelo loaded ... failures=0` for a plugin whose servers it
never registers — no error, no failed server, just an empty Customize list. Do
not treat that log line, or the presence of the files, as proof the plugin works.
The only evidence that settles it is a `plugin-angelo-<server>` entry in Cursor's
own MCP log, which `angelo doctor` now reports for you.

1. Run `angelo doctor` and read the registration line —
   `Cursor registered N plugin server(s): ...`. That reports what Cursor *did*;
   every other check only reports what angelo *wrote*. If it says Cursor
   registered none (and the editor has been restarted since installing), the
   plugin is loading but exposing nothing — go to step 3.
2. Confirm `~/.cursor/plugins/local/angelo/` exists and is a **real directory,
   not a symlink** — Cursor's loader rejects symlinked local plugins (even though
   Cursor's own docs suggest `ln -s`). If it is missing, re-run `angelo init` or
   `angelo doctor --fix`.
3. Confirm the **layout**, which Cursor requires exactly:

   ```
   ~/.cursor/plugins/local/angelo/
     .cursor-plugin/plugin.json    ← "mcpServers": ".cursor/mcp.json"
     .cursor/mcp.json              ← the server definitions
   ```

   The manifest must sit under `.cursor-plugin/`, and its `mcpServers` must be a
   *relative path string* — not the server objects inlined. A manifest at the
   plugin root, or one with inlined servers, registers nothing. Repair with
   `angelo update` (or `angelo doctor --fix`), which rewrites the layout and
   removes a stale root-level `plugin.json`.
4. Restart the editor so the loader re-scans the plugins directory.

Because the plugin is user-global it loads in every workspace, so each angelo MCP
server **self-gates**: in a project that isn't an angelo project it stands down and
does nothing. Pre-3.9 Cursor has no Customize inventory and loads
`.cursor/mcp.json` directly.

## Restart memory without restarting Cursor (opt-in)

The default install remains stdio-first. When Cursor cannot restart an individual
stdio server, `angelo memory http start` launches the existing memory server over
Streamable HTTP on `127.0.0.1`, verifies it, and atomically switches only
`mcpServers.memory` to that URL. Coordinator, artifacts, zettelkasten, Claude
templates, and future `angelo update` runs remain on their normal stdio paths.

- `angelo memory http status` reports inactive, running, stale/dead, or config-conflict state.
- `angelo memory http restart` replaces only the recorded daemon on the same port.
- `angelo memory http stop` stops it and restores the exact pre-pilot memory entry.
- `angelo doctor` reports pilot health but never auto-starts it.
- `angelo kill` may leave intentionally detectable stale state; use `restart` or `stop`.

The pilot is loopback-only: do not expose it remotely. If a user edits
`mcpServers.memory` after activation, `stop` fails closed and keeps both the edit
and the original backup in `.angelo/memory-http.json` rather than overwriting
either.

## Local edits to managed rules survive updates

`angelo update` is **non-destructive to files you edited.** It tracks the version it installed in a committed manifest (`.cursor/angelo-manifest.json`, and `.claude/angelo-manifest.json` for the Claude layer):

- A managed file you never touched is refreshed to the new bundled version (normal upgrade).
- A managed file you edited is **never silently overwritten.** Commit the manifest along with `.cursor/` so the baseline travels with the repo (a fresh clone on another machine then knows which files are pristine vs. edited).

**When `angelo update` finds an edited managed Cursor rule**, it offers to move your changes into a matching local override:

- **Interactive (a TTY):** it prompts per edited rule — `[m]` migrate your edits into `<name>-local.mdc` and refresh the managed rule (recommended), `[k]` keep your edits and write the upstream version as `<file>.new`, or `[o]` overwrite (discard your edits). For non-rule files only `[k]`/`[o]` are offered.
- **Non-interactive:** pass `--migrate-edits` to auto-migrate edited Cursor rules, or `--keep-edits` to always keep + write `.new`. With neither flag and no TTY (CI, scripts), it keeps your edits and writes `.new` (safe default).

**Migration** extracts the lines you added relative to the version you installed, appends them as a dated block to `.cursor/rules/<name>-local.mdc` (an un-managed rule Cursor loads alongside the managed one, with `alwaysApply: true`), then refreshes the managed rule to the new upstream and re-baselines it. Review the migrated block and trim it to just your customizations.

**Prefer a separate, un-managed rule for customizations from the start.** If you keep project-specific coordinator behavior (e.g. instructions for your custom agents) in its own `*-local.mdc` from the beginning, `angelo update` never has anything to migrate — it loads alongside the managed rule but is outside angelo's managed set, so it is never touched.

## After any setup change

Restart the editor so it picks up new code, rules, the user-global Cursor plugin,
or `mcp.json` changes. In Cursor 3.9+, inspect **Customize → MCPs**; older Cursor
releases continue using `.cursor/mcp.json`. The running server holds the old
state in memory until restarted.
